<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    What may leave this phone in a backup or a device transfer, on Android
    12 and later (ADR 0019 § 4).

    **`filesDir/sync` is excluded, and that is what makes the ADR's
    re-enrolment rule mechanical.** § 4 keeps the device identity outside
    the vault precisely so that a restored copy is a new device: the vault
    is a portable artefact that gets exported and backed up, and an
    identity travelling with it is "a cloned identity with a shared
    `op_seq` space". Android's default backup would have carried the
    identity anyway — through the app's own private storage rather than
    through the vault — and undone that by the back door.

    Two things would then have gone wrong on a restored phone, and one of
    them is worse than the other:

      - The identity file is sealed by an AndroidKeyStore key, and Keystore
        keys are **not** backed up. So the file arrives on the new phone
        and nothing on it can open the file: enrolment fails with "the
        Keystore key that sealed this device's identity is no longer on
        this phone", and sync is unavailable until somebody clears it by
        hand. That is the state the Sync screen's "Reset sync on this
        phone" exists to recover from, and this file is why it should stop
        happening.
      - Had the key been restorable, the outcome would have been worse
        still: two phones holding one identity and one sequence space,
        which is the fork the threat model means when it says sequence
        reuse is never legitimate.

    With the directory absent, the identity is simply not there, the kernel
    mints a fresh one, and "a restored phone is a new device" holds because
    of where the bytes are rather than because of a check somebody
    remembered to write.

    The vault itself deliberately stays in backup. A restored vault with no
    identity beside it is exactly § 4's third row: adopt nothing, mint a
    new identity and a new device id, start a fresh sequence space. The
    person's notes come back; the device is new; the peers are re-paired.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="file" path="sync" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="file" path="sync" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
